Here is the problem description:
<description>
Sometimes one has to spell email addresses over the phone. Then one usually pronounces a dot as dot, an at sign as at. As a result, we get something like vasyaatgmaildotcom. Your task is to transform it into a proper email address (vasya@gmail.com). It is known that a proper email address contains only such symbols as . @ and lower-case Latin letters, doesn't start with and doesn't end with a dot. Also, a proper email address doesn't start with and doesn't end with an at sign. Moreover, an email address contains exactly one such symbol as @, yet may contain any number (possible, zero) of dots. You have to carry out a series of replacements so that the length of the result was as short as possible and it was a proper email address. If the lengths are equal, you should print the lexicographically minimal result. Overall, two variants of replacement are possible: dot can be replaced by a dot, at can be replaced by an at. 
</description>

Here is the input specification:
<input_spec>
The first line contains the email address description. It is guaranteed that that is a proper email address with all the dots replaced by dot an the at signs replaced by at. The line is not empty and its length does not exceed 100 symbols.
</input_spec>


Here are sample inputs:
<sample_inputs>
<input>
vasyaatgmaildotcom
</input>
<input>
dotdotdotatdotdotat
</input>
<input>
aatt
</input>
</sample_inputs>

Here is the source code:
<source_code>
#include "bits/stdc++.h"
using namespace std;

const int maxn = 3e5 + 10;
char str[maxn];
int N;

string solve() {
    cin >> str + 1;
    N = strlen(str + 1);

    // dp[i] = set of valid prefixes of length i
    vector<set<string>> dp(N + 1);
    dp[0].insert("");

    for (int i = 1; i <= N; ++i) {
        // Option 1: add current character as a letter
        for (const string& s : dp[i-1]) {
            dp[i].insert(s + str[i]);
        }

        // Option 2: check for "at" -> '@'
        if (i >= 2 && str[i-1] == 'a' && str[i] == 't') {
            for (const string& s : dp[i-2]) {
                // '@' cannot be first or last, and we can only have one '@'
                if (s.empty()) continue;
                int at_count = count(s.begin(), s.end(), '@');
                if (at_count == 0) {
                    dp[i].insert(s + '@');
                }
            }
        }

        // Option 3: check for "dot" -> '.'
        if (i >= 3 && str[i-2] == 'd' && str[i-1] == 'o' && str[i] == 't') {
            for (const string& s : dp[i-3]) {
                // '.' cannot be first or last
                if (s.empty()) continue;
                int at_count = count(s.begin(), s.end(), '@');
                // We can add '.' as long as it's not at the end (we'll check final validity later)
                dp[i].insert(s + '.');
            }
        }
    }

    // Filter valid answers
    vector<string> valid;
    for (const string& s : dp[N]) {
        if (s.empty()) continue;
        // Must have exactly one '@'
        if (count(s.begin(), s.end(), '@') != 1) continue;
        // Cannot start or end with '@' or '.'
        if (s.front() == '@' || s.front() == '.' || s.back() == '@' || s.back() == '.') continue;
        valid.push_back(s);
    }

    if (valid.empty()) {
        // Fallback: try to construct manually if DP didn't find solution
        // This handles edge cases where our DP might miss some possibilities
        // But per problem statement, input should always have a solution
        return "";
    }

    sort(valid.begin(), valid.end());
    return valid[0];
}

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(0);

    cout << solve() << endl;

    return 0;
}
</source_code>
